Non-FAT file systems and the resident XUSB mailbox service
==========================================================

1. File systems (`-D SWITCH_FSDRV=1 -D SWITCH_BIGFD=1`)
   Twenty-five read-only AArch64 UEFI drivers from upstream pbatard/EfiFs v1.13 (GRUB 2.16 code, GPLv3+) are embedded
   in the firmware volume as unmodified binaries under `efi_drivers/efifs-1.13-aa64/`. `NintendoSwitch.fdf` lists them;
   `SHA256SUMS` records each binary hash. The set includes NTFS/exFAT (Windows), ext2/3/4, XFS, Btrfs, JFS, ReiserFS,
   NILFS2, SquashFS (Linux), F2FS/EROFS (Android/Linux), HFS/HFS+, UFS1/UFS2, ZFS, ISO9660, UDF, ROMFS, CBFS, SFS,
   TAR and CPIO. FAT/FAT32 uses EDK II FatDxe. APFS is not included.
   These drivers expose read-only Simple File System volumes over BlockIo; they do not provide OS kernel or device drivers.
   The Switch SD stack is read-only too. `SWITCH_BIGFD` is required for this firmware profile. Distributing firmware with
   EfiFs binaries requires preserving their GPLv3+ licensing/source offer obligations.
   The remaining 11 upstream formats can be built as standalone read-only AArch64 EFI drivers with
   `Tools/build-efifs-extras.sh`; see `docs/multiboot/efifs-os-capability-ja.md` for setup and boot limits.

2. XUSB resident mailbox (shell command `xusbinit`, SWITCH_XUSB)
   Before: `xusbinit svc` answered the xHCI firmware's clock requests only while the command ran (30 s).
   Now:    `xusbinit up [xusb.bin]`  = clk, pad, ss, fw, ports, then a timer daemon (10 ms) that keeps answering
           until ExitBootServices.  `xusbinit daemon [stop]`, `xusbinit dlog` (what it logged).
   Still missing (not done): a PCI-IO emulation so EDK2's stock XhciDxe can drive XUSB, and an OS-side
   mailbox handler (after ExitBootServices nothing answers the firmware). Untested on hardware.
